<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><language>en</language><title>Breaking news from AFRICA, Maghreb and Sub-Saharan Africa - France 24</title><description>FRANCE 24’s coverage of events, politics and social issues across Africa, including North Africa (the Maghreb) and the Sahel, aided by our network of correspondents and experts across the continent.</description><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/</link><lastBuildDate>Mon, 23 Nov 2026 07:50:54 GMT</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><category>Africa</category><title>Russian warship hit by Ukrainian drone at Benghazi naval base, Libyan officials say</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261122-russian-warship-hit-by-suspected-ukrainian-drone-at-libya-s-benghazi-naval-base</link><description>
A Russian navy corvette docked at the Benghazi naval base ​in eastern Libya was hit on Sunday by a Ukrainian sea drone, according to local officials, hours before a cargo ship was also hit by a sea drone in a second attack. Ukraine's President Volodymyr Zelensky on Monday confirmed that his country's military was behind the strikes.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/659e3cc8-e6a9-11f1-ae55-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26326649718247.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/659e3cc8-e6a9-11f1-ae55-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26326649718247.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">59c27282-e6a1-11f1-8d36-99870da4d23e</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 19:41:23 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/659e3cc8-e6a9-11f1-ae55-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26326649718247.jpg">© AP via Vanto satellite image</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Drone strikes hit homes and UN building in Sudan's Khartoum</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261122-drone-strikes-hit-homes-and-un-building-in-sudan-s-khartoum</link><description>
Drone strikes damaged residential homes and a United Nations building in Sudan's capital Khartoum early Sunday, with the army accusing neighbouring Ethiopia of launching the attacks. The strikes came as drone warfare intensifies in the country's conflict, now in its fourth year. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1c5eb51c-e693-11f1-9b46-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/5b43f69bec605872bb956153617567218ceba021.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1c5eb51c-e693-11f1-9b46-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/5b43f69bec605872bb956153617567218ceba021.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1d39bae0-e693-11f1-a9ff-1deb7bcd9931</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 16:38:14 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1c5eb51c-e693-11f1-9b46-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/5b43f69bec605872bb956153617567218ceba021.jpg">© Ebrahim Hamid, AFP </source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Ethiopia: Tackling period poverty and stigma</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-tackling-period-poverty-and-stigma</link><description><![CDATA[
In Ethiopia, entrepreneur Hanan Ahmed is tackling period poverty and the stigma surrounding menstruation. By producing affordable reusable sanitary pads and raising awareness in schools, she hopes to make menstrual health more accessible and help girls stay in class. Report by Awa Diouf & Guillaume Gougeon.
]]></description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2a9827a2-e5a4-11f1-8650-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261120-215007-215136-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2a9827a2-e5a4-11f1-8650-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261120-215007-215136-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">2f6b1c62-e5a4-11f1-9016-8169673db442</guid><pubDate>Sat, 21 Nov 2026 12:22:44 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Guinea bans Burkina Faso broadcaster, escalating diplomatic tensions</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20261120-guinea-bans-burkina-faso-broadcaster-escalating-diplomatic-tensions</link><description>
Tensions rise between Guinea and Burkina Faso after Conakry bans Burkinabe broadcaster RTB, amid a diplomatic dispute over gold miners and a caricature of President Mamadi Doumbouya. Meanwhile, fighting erupts between Congolese forces and the Rwanda-backed FRN group in eastern DR Congo, despite peace commitments. Finally, in Ethiopia, one entrepreneur is tackling period poverty by breaking menstrual stigma and making reusable sanitary pads more accessible.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8e31d6f6-e52b-11f1-9535-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-a6bd8a5c.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8e31d6f6-e52b-11f1-9535-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-a6bd8a5c.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4a97e566-e52b-11f1-9c5f-9fcf7db39ba3</guid><pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 21:53:35 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clémence WALLER</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Kenyan marathon champion Peres Jepchirchir banned for doping</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20261119-kenyan-marathon-champion-banned-for-doping</link><description>
In tonight's edition: Kenyan athletics superstar and reigning marathon world champion Peres Jepchirchir is banned for three years for doping. Also, South African judges leave the courtroom behind and tour a repatriation centre before handing down judgment in 33 emergency asylum applications. Plus Ghana's presidency has a new jet, but critics of the ruling party are not on board. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/42bd4652-e4c1-11f1-ac8d-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-f05f83d7.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/42bd4652-e4c1-11f1-ac8d-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-f05f83d7.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">dec457cc-e460-11f1-83ea-db85787c2904</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 21:38:55 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category>Tête à tête</category><title>US has 'unbalanced approach' on DR Congo deal, Rwanda's foreign minister says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t%C3%AAte-%C3%A0-t%C3%AAte/20261119-us-has-unbalanced-approach-on-dr-congo-deal-rwanda-s-foreign-minister-says</link><description>
In an interview with FRANCE 24, Rwandan Foreign Minister Olivier Nduhungirehe said the Trump administration had "an unbalanced approach" in implementing the DR Congo peace deal it brokered. He also said that the recent row over the Organisation Internationale de la Francophonie's top job was "manufactured in Paris and Kinshasa". Finally, he defended a deal for Rwanda to host failed asylum seekers from five European countries.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1da1c348-e417-11f1-91e2-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/FR-NW-PKG-CLEAN-EN-TETE-A-TETE-Olivier-J-P-Nduhungirehe-AUBIN-Sophian.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1da1c348-e417-11f1-91e2-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/FR-NW-PKG-CLEAN-EN-TETE-A-TETE-Olivier-J-P-Nduhungirehe-AUBIN-Sophian.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3e342fd8-e42b-11f1-8816-c724df93fed8</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 17:07:09 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Marc PERELMAN</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Nigeria: States set to get their own police forces</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/nigeria-states-set-to-get-their-own-police-forces</link><description>
Nigeria has moved a step closer to letting its 36 states set up their own police forces. The National Assembly has sent a constitutional amendment bill to the president, as authorities seek state-run police to complement the federal force amid growing insecurity. Many states currently fund vigilante groups to fight armed groups like Boko Haram, but these groups are poorly trained and ill-equipped for the task. FRANCE 24's correspondent has the latest.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/85e5a052-e410-11f1-8151-6b451f569414/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261118-214527-214725-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/85e5a052-e410-11f1-8151-6b451f569414/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261118-214527-214725-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8aa44116-e410-11f1-995e-c3a586aca863</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:20:35 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Senegal: Pastef removes President Faye from its ranks</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/senegal-pastef-removes-president-faye-from-its-ranks</link><description>
In Senegal, Pastef, the majority party led by National Assembly president Ousmane Sonko, has made official the departure of President Bassirou Diomaye Faye. It is the latest episode in a split with his former Prime minister that was already largely complete, and it comes in the middle of an economic crisis.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a851556a-e40f-11f1-b1b5-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261118-214326-214524-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a851556a-e40f-11f1-b1b5-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261118-214326-214524-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ad72f0d0-e40f-11f1-aee3-d1fc0af3cb61</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:05:40 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>President Bassirou Diomaye Faye excluded from Senegal's ruling party</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/bassirou-diomaye-faye-excluded-from-senegal-s-ruling-party</link><description>
In tonight's edition: The political divorce between Senegal's president and his powerful former prime minister and mentor is official. Also, all of Nigeria's states have shown willingness for a push to create their own police forces. Plus we see how in parts of the continent, conflict and a drop in funding have placed huge pressure on children's learning. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a145d098-e41e-11f1-9507-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-a317f3ba.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a145d098-e41e-11f1-9507-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-a317f3ba.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">10fc3a7e-e395-11f1-8fb8-2356d0896181</guid><pubDate>Wed, 18 Nov 2026 21:21:28 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category>Focus</category><title>Chinese cars shake up Ivory Coast's auto industry</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261118-chinese-cars-shake-up-ivory-coast-s-auto-industry</link><description>
In the space of just a few years, more than a dozen Chinese carmakers have established themselves in Ivory Coast and are becoming increasingly visible on the country's streets. How have they managed to win over a growing number of customers? FRANCE 24's Jean Barrère and Eunice Stolz-Masson report.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b764e906-e34a-11f1-82d5-ff16da94d1fe/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-9e1e00a0.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b764e906-e34a-11f1-82d5-ff16da94d1fe/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-9e1e00a0.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">68e7eeb0-e2ad-11f1-917c-0b2e0a4e23f7</guid><pubDate>Wed, 18 Nov 2026 12:25:36 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Jean BARRERE</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>AI imagery complicates Nigeria's election preparations</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/ai-imagery-complicates-nigeria-s-election-prep</link><description>
In tonight's edition: Concerns emerge that artificial intelligence has been used to generate fake voting profiles ahead of Nigeria's high-stakes general election in January. Also, Nigeria's mining marshal heads underground to unearth some of the people and organisations behind the vast illegal mining industries. Plus a groundbreaking archaeological discovery in northern Sudan could be the ancient site of the largest church ever built in one of medieval Nubia's most important Christian sites. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/65b601b6-e32d-11f1-b8b0-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-82f8fe31.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/65b601b6-e32d-11f1-b8b0-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-82f8fe31.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4e7b18ee-e2cc-11f1-a02c-ff1458ed0a1b</guid><pubDate>Tue, 17 Nov 2026 21:20:27 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category>Spotlight</category><title>Predictable: DR Congo-Rwanda rivalry spills over to Francophonie summit</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261116-la-francophonie-waning-in-global-influence-linguistic-institutions-becoming-less-important</link><description>
François Picard is pleased to welcome Phil Clark, professor of international politics at SOAS University of London. The diplomatic crisis between Rwanda and DR Congo spilling out at the Francophonie summit in Phnom Penh has exposed an unexpected shift in their relations. Clark says that "nobody expected Rwanda to back down in this way" following President Emmanuel Macron's request that President Paul Kagame withdraw Louise Mushikiwabo's candidacy for a third term as head of the group.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/6832fb3c-e298-11f1-bd8e-ff16da94d1fe/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-2cf9efa9.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/6832fb3c-e298-11f1-bd8e-ff16da94d1fe/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-2cf9efa9.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3a0c84c0-e20d-11f1-913b-771c1de741c0</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 22:29:45 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>François PICARD</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Rwanda to host hub for rejected EU migrants</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/rwanda-to-host-hub-for-rejected-eu-migrants</link><description>
In tonight's edition: Five European countries are negotiating a deal to deport rejected asylum seekers to Rwanda. Also, DR Congo and Rwanda's political animosity spills over at the Francophonie Summit in Cambodia. Plus in Tunisia, young women are increasingly turning towards weightlifting and bodybuilding as a form of self-expression.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b210f7d0-e27b-11f1-83ae-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-4250847b.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b210f7d0-e27b-11f1-83ae-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-4250847b.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4854abbc-e202-11f1-a03e-fdd6321e2ad6</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 21:21:14 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clémence WALLER</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Francophonie summit postpones new leader vote amid DR Congo-Rwanda dispute</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261116-francophonie-summit-postpones-new-leader-vote-amid-dr-congo-rwanda-dispute</link><description>
The Organisation Internationale de la Francophonie (OIF) on Monday had been set to choose its new secretary general during talks in the Cambodian capital Phnom Penh. But delegates decided to postpone the election for a year after a walkout threat by the Democratic Republic of Congo amid an ongoing dispute between Kinshasa and Kigali.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3df16a22-e188-11f1-be7e-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D7HJ4YA.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3df16a22-e188-11f1-be7e-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D7HJ4YA.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8b068e9a-e1a7-11f1-a0c0-95c7966141b5</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 10:51:40 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3df16a22-e188-11f1-be7e-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D7HJ4YA.jpg">© Tang Chin Sothy, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Five EU nations unveil migrant deportation centre deal with Rwanda</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261116-eu-nations-to-unveil-rwanda-deal-for-migrant-return-hub</link><description>
Five EU countries on Monday announced a deal to establish a migrant deportation centre in Rwanda – the first such facility outside the bloc. The move comes after the EU toughened its migration rules in late 2025.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fc6e005a-e183-11f1-946e-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/9521062135528b281502a69403234cbad4e4369f.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fc6e005a-e183-11f1-946e-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/9521062135528b281502a69403234cbad4e4369f.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">fd4b0b6c-e183-11f1-814f-27463419402b</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 06:07:22 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fc6e005a-e183-11f1-946e-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/9521062135528b281502a69403234cbad4e4369f.jpg">© Anne-Christine Poujoulat, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Canaries urge government action after 2,200 migrants reach the islands in 48h</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261115-canaries-urge-government-action-after-2-200-migrants-reach-the-islands-in-48h-1</link><description>
Spain says nearly 2,200 migrants have arrived by boat to the Canary Islands this weekend. The Spanish islands are one of the main entry points into Europe for migrants from Africa. The Spanish Interior Ministry says more than 13,000 arrived there between January and the end of last month. Details and analysis by Andrew Hilliar, FRANCE 24 international affairs editor.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/6524e48c-e108-11f1-a387-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261115-160718-161132-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/6524e48c-e108-11f1-a387-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261115-160718-161132-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6a1a4c02-e108-11f1-ad35-f165ee9aaa0a</guid><pubDate>Sun, 15 Nov 2026 15:24:58 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>A deep dive into Ivory Coast's solar energy goals</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/a-deep-dive-into-ivory-coast-s-solar-energy-goals</link><description>
Can Ivory Coast, which remains heavily dependent on fossil fuels, turn to solar power to make its energy consumption greener and more sustainable? We visited the country's new solar power plant in Ferkessédougou, in the north of the country. Inaugurated in July, it is expected to generate nearly 90 gigawatt-hours of electricity a year. Report by Julia Guggenheim.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a07102cc-e0ff-11f1-86c8-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261115-081217-081424-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a07102cc-e0ff-11f1-86c8-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261115-081217-081424-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">a5fb1a84-e0ff-11f1-a55d-efe590fb5315</guid><pubDate>Sun, 15 Nov 2026 14:25:24 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Cape Verde's President José Maria Neves wins second term</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261115-cape-verde-could-make-election-history-with-first-female-president</link><description>
Cape Verde's President Jose Maria Neves won re-election on Sunday with 63.8 percent of votes cast, provisional electoral commission results showed. Neves had been the favourite in the West African archipelago. His main challenger was Joana Rosa Amado, the country's first woman presidential candidate, who conceded and congratulated Neves Monday morning.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f7912c6a-e183-11f1-92a7-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26316770477848.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f7912c6a-e183-11f1-92a7-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26316770477848.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">da234acc-e0c6-11f1-b715-0916f3da5609</guid><pubDate>Sun, 15 Nov 2026 07:56:14 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f7912c6a-e183-11f1-92a7-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26316770477848.jpg">© Cristiano Barbos, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Youth Olympic Games organisers hail Senegal's capability to host major events</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/youth-olympic-games-organisers-hail-senegal-s-capability-to-host-major-events</link><description>
Dakar 2026 is over. Two weeks of competition, thousands of young athletes and a historic first for Africa. The Youth Olympic Games brought the world to Senegal for the first Olympic sporting event ever held on the continent. From record-breaking performances to historic medals for the host nation, Dakar 2026 has been a celebration of sport, youth and cultural exchange. Our correspondent Aminatou Diallo reports from Dakar.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/22d2e2f4-e050-11f1-b0c8-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-14T101501Z-1652258025-RC2ZMOAAAUIS-RTRMADP-3-OLYMPICS-YOUTH.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/22d2e2f4-e050-11f1-b0c8-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-14T101501Z-1652258025-RC2ZMOAAAUIS-RTRMADP-3-OLYMPICS-YOUTH.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6482931a-e024-11f1-a8e1-092eac3c8fd8</guid><pubDate>Sat, 14 Nov 2026 17:25:51 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>DR Congo, Rwanda clash over Francophonie leadership as Macron seeks compromise</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261114-dr-congo-rwanda-clash-over-francophonie-leadership-as-macron-seeks-compromise</link><description>
DR Congo and Rwanda clashed Saturday over the leadership of the Francophonie, as tensions between the neighbouring countries spilled into the race to head the 53-member organisation. French President Emmanuel Macron urged Rwanda to withdraw its candidate in favour of a compromise choice ahead of Monday's vote.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1dfdc724-e039-11f1-b629-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/000-36JD2YF.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1dfdc724-e039-11f1-b629-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/000-36JD2YF.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">b57f6e5a-e038-11f1-98ff-014aaf8109a4</guid><pubDate>Sat, 14 Nov 2026 14:54:43 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1dfdc724-e039-11f1-b629-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/000-36JD2YF.jpg">© Ludovic Marin, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Inside Ethiopia's war: Rare access to a hospital treating victims of renewed fighting</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/inside-ethiopia-s-war-rare-access-to-a-hospital-treating-victims-of-renewed-fighting</link><description>
Ethiopia's renewed conflict is taking an increasing toll on civilians, with humanitarian organisations reporting a rise in casualties. In the Amhara region, FRANCE 24’s correspondent Tom Canetti was granted rare access to a civilian hospital where surgeons are struggling to treat the influx of wounded patients. But for many caught in the fighting, reaching medical care is a challenge in itself.
 
Warning: The report contains images of surgery that may be confronting for some viewers.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f5f45c22-e022-11f1-9671-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261114-090947-091144-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f5f45c22-e022-11f1-9671-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261114-090947-091144-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">fb429b9e-e022-11f1-89f0-03213c6d6be3</guid><pubDate>Sat, 14 Nov 2026 12:23:32 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Gambia's Adama Barrow seeks third term</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/gambia-s-adama-barrow-seeks-third-term</link><description>
In tonight's programme, Gambia's electoral commission has announced five official candidates for the country's upcoming presidential election including incumbent Adama Barrow who is seeking a third term. Tensions continue to rise in Ethiopia following the federal government's appointment of a new head of the Western Tigray region. And the Dakar Youth Olympic Games come to a close after two weeks of intense competition.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5ffd1078-dfab-11f1-ae95-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-43ee1b88.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5ffd1078-dfab-11f1-ae95-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-43ee1b88.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">9812de26-dfaa-11f1-a1ec-6563a7224ff5</guid><pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:46:32 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Hélène Daouphars</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Guinea opens illegal gold mining trial, 1,200 people arrested</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261113-guinea-opens-illegal-gold-mining-trial-1-200-people-arrested</link><description>
Guinea opened a trial over illegal gold mining on Thursday, November 12, after a crackdown on mineral extraction in the district of Siguiri. There was a ban on "unregulated and illegal" gold mining in the area where the military was subsequently deployed. Security forces have expelled tens of thousands of foreign nationals who had come to work in the region's mines.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/de9a7698-df4a-11f1-8c8d-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-485db877.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/de9a7698-df4a-11f1-8c8d-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-485db877.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">588ba23c-df47-11f1-8ce9-033433160d86</guid><pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 10:28:40 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Toera's homecoming: Madagascar's slain king joins his ancestors after 129 years</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261113-toera-s-homecoming-madagascar-s-slain-king-joins-his-ancestors-after-129-years</link><description>
In 1897, French colonial troops killed King Toera, ruler of Madagascar's powerful Sakalava kingdom of Menabe, and took his skull to Paris, where it remained hidden in a museum for more than a century. France returned the remains last year, and for the first time since his death, King Toera took his place among his ancestors during the Sakalava people's most sacred ritual.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/550445c6-df4a-11f1-907a-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-2370f1b2.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/550445c6-df4a-11f1-907a-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-2370f1b2.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">76c6ea08-df48-11f1-a8b6-2f2e3f8027e2</guid><pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 10:27:02 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Amnesty criticises South Sudan, UN over failure to heed massacre warnings</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261113-amnesty-criticises-south-sudan-un-over-failure-to-heed-massacre-warnings</link><description>
Amnesty International said Thursday, November 12, that South Sudan and UN peacekeepers failed to act on warnings of a massacre that ended with more than 200 dead in March. The world's youngest country has been plagued by civil war, poverty, corruption and intercommunal violence since it was formed in 2011. FRANCE 24 spoke to Donatella Rovera, Senior crisis response adviser at Amnesty International.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/69bdde8e-df48-11f1-b4c2-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-de14a891.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/69bdde8e-df48-11f1-b4c2-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-de14a891.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">476ce30e-df46-11f1-bfca-993e80f8d692</guid><pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 10:25:47 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Across Africa</category><title>Across Africa: Tunisians drawn into Russia's war with Ukraine</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/across-africa-tunisians-drawn-into-russia-s-war-with-ukraine</link><description>
This week: A new report says more than 1,400 Africans have been recruited to fight for Russia in Ukraine since 2023. Also, as South Africa races to become Africa's digital gateway, a growing backlash is emerging in Cape Town. Plus music, dance and theatre lit up Abéché as the city's arts festival in Chad drew crowds of young spectators.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/885cf2d4-dfa8-11f1-aa4c-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-8a0b686a.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/885cf2d4-dfa8-11f1-aa4c-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-8a0b686a.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4396d9f4-deb7-11f1-a47f-1fb8837136ad</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 21:26:17 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clarisse FORTUNÉ</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>NGO criticises UN's response to massacre in South Sudan</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/ngo-criticizes-un-s-response-to-massacre-in-south-sudan</link><description>
In tonight's edition: Amnesty International accuses both the government and UN peacekeepers of failing to protect civilians during a massacre in South Sudan that killed over 200 people. Also, more than a thousand people are prosecuted for illegal mining in Guinea. Plus a century after French colonial troops killed him, a Malagasy king has finally come home.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1b87e912-dfa7-11f1-b9cb-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-1c0cd171.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1b87e912-dfa7-11f1-b9cb-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-1c0cd171.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">56d72ef2-dede-11f1-817c-9944036d14be</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 21:20:52 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clarisse FORTUNÉ</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>DR Congo's Tshisekedi signs referendum law amid fears of third-term bid</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261112-dr-congo-s-tshisekedi-signs-referendum-law-amid-fears-of-third-term-bid</link><description>
DR Congo President Félix Tshisekedi paved the way for a constitutional amendment on November 10 by signing a law authorising a referendum. The move comes amid concerns that he could seek a third term in office, with opposition figures accusing him of manoeuvring to remain in power.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/48a9a32e-de7e-11f1-b569-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-5871cbff.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/48a9a32e-de7e-11f1-b569-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-5871cbff.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">fcd86860-de7b-11f1-8f9b-09afb001c0c0</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 10:51:57 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Kenya discharges last Ebola patient, 42-day countdown to 'Ebola-free' begins</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261112-kenya-discharges-last-ebola-patient-42-day-countdown-to-ebola-free-begins</link><description>
Kenya has discharged its last Ebola patient, who has now officially recovered. The 11 other people who had been in quarantine were also released from hospital on Wednesday, November 11, as the country began a 42-day countdown to being declared Ebola-free. FRANCE 24's Nairobi correspondent Bastien Renouil reports.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4e24b324-de7f-11f1-892b-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-32ee28fc.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4e24b324-de7f-11f1-892b-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-32ee28fc.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">83896b84-de7c-11f1-b769-056bc714d151</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 10:48:51 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>COP31: Experts call for zero waste, as Africa grapples with mounting garbage</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261112-cop31-experts-call-for-zero-waste-as-africa-grapples-with-mounting-garbage</link><description>
As the COP31 climate summit gets under way this week in Turkey, experts are calling for zero-waste solutions to tackle Africa's growing waste crisis. As governments search for ways to address the crisis, a new report warns of a growing push for waste-to-energy incineration across the continent. FRANCE 24 spoke to Mponwe Minette, founder and executive director of Soft Power Global and a climate activist.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/25c97044-de80-11f1-8c9f-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-ebf89a3c.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/25c97044-de80-11f1-8c9f-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-ebf89a3c.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1316419a-de7e-11f1-ba49-8fd66c42d8ce</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 10:45:19 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item></channel></rss>
